#!/bin/sh
# Two independent jobs, each silent when not relevant:
#   1. narration mode, if the flag file is there
#   2. live audio state, if something is being spoken right now
K="${KOKORO_HOME:-$HOME/.kokoro}"

if [ -f "$K/.narrate" ]; then
cat <<'TXT'
<narration-mode>
Narration mode is ON. Load the `narrate` skill before your first beat and follow
it. In short: speak brief progress beats with `speak --bg "..."` as you work
(three to eight for a normal task; never plain `speak`, which blocks), and queue a
spoken summary the same way when you finish.

NEVER speak a filename, path or extension aloud - "player dot g d" is nonsense in
the ear. Name what the file DOES: "the player movement script", "the spring
script". Same for code, commands, flags and long numbers.

Keep answering in the chat as normal; this is an extra channel, not a
replacement. If the user wants it to stop, run `speak --hush` and remove the
narration flag at "$KOKORO_HOME/.narrate" (default ~/.kokoro/.narrate).
</narration-mode>
TXT
fi

[ -f "$K/.now-playing" ] || exit 0
KOKORO_STATE="$K" python3 - <<'PY'
import json, os, pathlib, time, sys
k = pathlib.Path(os.environ["KOKORO_STATE"])
try:
    st = json.loads((k/".now-playing").read_text())
except Exception:
    sys.exit(0)
if time.time() - st.get("at", 0) > 120:
    sys.exit(0)
s, i = st["sentences"], st["index"]
print("<speaking-now>")
print(f"You are speaking aloud RIGHT NOW - sentence {i+1} of {st['total']}.")
print("The user is hearing this as they type. What they just heard:")
for n, line in enumerate(s[max(0, i-3): i+1], start=max(0, i-3)):
    print(f"  {'>>' if n == i else '  '} {line}")
if (k/".paused").exists():
    print("Playback is currently PAUSED.")
print("""
Their message may well be about what they are hearing - a request to slow down,
go back, explain, or stop. It may equally be unrelated. Judge it; do not pattern
match on particular words.

If it IS about the audio: run `speak --pause` FIRST, before anything else,
because every second you think is another sentence they sit through. Then answer
in the chat as text - they asked because listening did not work, so repeating it
aloud will not help. Then `speak --resume` to carry on from exactly where it
froze, or `speak --hush` to drop the rest if they have moved on. `speak --where`
gives more context than the window above.

If it is NOT about the audio, ignore this block entirely and let it play.
</speaking-now>""")
PY
